{
  "dom_id": "step-q65855",
  "name": "Question 13",
  "problem": "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the exact solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{tan}\u20612x=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}$ for $0\\le x<\\pi ,$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}?$",
  "worked_solution": "We will find the general solution to the equation, and then generate all of the solutions in the required domain.\n\nThe general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & =-\\frac{\\pi }{6}+n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the equation above for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & =-\\frac{\\pi }{6}+n\\pi  \\\\ x & =-\\frac{\\pi }{12}+\\frac{n\\pi }{2} \\\\ x & =\\frac{(6n-1)\\pi }{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,\u2026,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain $0\\le x<\\pi .$\n\n- Setting $n=1$ gives $x=\\frac{5\\pi }{12}$\n\n- Setting $n=2$ gives $x=\\frac{11\\pi }{12}$\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain $0\\le x<\\pi .$ So, the solutions are $x=\\frac{5\\pi }{12},\\frac{11\\pi }{12}.$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=\\frac{5\\pi }{12}+\\frac{11\\pi }{12}=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}.\n$$",
